error[ffi_parameter_type]: `p` of `slot_pointer` is declared `ptr` and crosses by value, and `fixedbugs-2421-variadic.h` gives `slot_pointer` 1 parameter and then `...`, where `p` stands — C reads what a call hands it there as whatever type it asks for, so nothing holds it to the `ptr` this program hands it
  at tests/golden/unsupported/fixedbugs-2421-a-pointer-a-handle-and-a-callback-past-a-variadic-prototype-are-refused.hero:25:39
     |
  25 |     function slot_pointer(count: i32, p: ptr) -> i64  #~ ffi_parameter_type
     |                                       ^
  note: spec § 13: a parameter is declared at the header's own width and sign, and one that disagrees is refused. C11 6.5.2.2p7: past a prototype's `...` a call converts nothing beyond the default argument promotions, so `slot_pointer` reads the bytes as the type its `va_arg` names: an `i32` read as a C `long` gives -1 as 4294967295, and a wider integer, a float, a string, a pointer or a record read as another type is the same mistake
  note: write a header of your own that includes `fixedbugs-2421-variadic.h` and defines a `static inline` function of a new name with a fixed prototype, taking `p` at the C type `slot_pointer` reads there and calling `slot_pointer` with it; bind that function instead, and its prototype holds it to that type; where `slot_pointer` reads a format, write the format inside that function as a literal, and clang reads each conversion against the argument it is given

